    "innovation": "This paper uses climate models to show that a vegetated and less dusty Sahara during the mid-Holocene suppressed ENSO variability far more than orbital forcing alone, by strengthening the West African Monsoon which in turn altered tropical Atlantic conditions and the global Walker circulation.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Greening of the Sahara",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Sahara",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The evolution of the El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation (ENSO) during the Holocene remains uncertain. In particular, a host of new paleoclimate records suggest that ENSO internal variability or other external forcings may have dwarfed the fairly modest ENSO response to precessional insolation changes simulated in climate models. Here, using fully coupled ocean- atmosphere model simulations, we show that accounting for a vegetated and less dusty Sahara during the mid-Holocene relative to preindustrial climate can reduce ENSO variability by 25%, more than twice the decrease obtained using orbital forcing alone.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
